Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the legend of zelda is now  35 years old a little over one year  older than i am  look at that wasn't i cute no look at  that mullet  anyway to celebrate the anniversary i  want to do a quick video about the game  that started it all the legend of zelda  on nes from 1986.  now this isn't going to be like a  history video about the development of  the game there are plenty of videos out  there who've done an excellent job of  telling that story this is more like a  little personal tribute  despite almost sharing a birthday with  the nes classic  i actually didn't play this game until  many many years later  my first experience with the series was  ocarina of time and before that time  i was more of a mario kid my mom used to  work at a local video store and for  those who were born  after netflix was the place to be if you  wanted to get your hands on a good movie  or some other types of entertainment if  you know what i mean  this included video games as well you  could even rent the console itself to go  with it  and since i was still very young around  4 years if i recall correctly my mom  sometimes had to take me to work with  her whenever there was no one around to  babysit me  naturally this was very boring for a 4  year old since i wasn't allowed to just  roam around the store and potentially  break stuff or  walk into the wrong section of the store  alright enough of that so they had this  little back room behind the counter  where the  employees would go on their coffee break  and such which is where i had to stay  while my mom was on her shift  lucky for me the back room had an nes  setup and this little room is literally  where my love for video games started i  would play the original super mario  brothers for  hours upon end and it wasn't long until  i got my own system at home then the  super nintendo  n64 and with the exception of the  virtual boy every other nintendo console  since then  after finishing ocarina of time and  majora's mask we had to wait a while  before the wind waker would finally show  up so in the meantime i decided to pick  up some of the older games which  included the original and one of the  first things i noticed was  this game is hard as balls now don't get  me wrong the first mario wasn't the  easiest to complete  either especially for a 4 year old but  at least it was easy to comprehend  move to the right don't touch enemies  don't fall to your death there are some  secrets and occasional riddles here and  there but almost none of it is mandatory  it just makes the game easier you also  didn't have to worry about grinding  finding better gear or anything like  that zelda on the other hand was all  about secrets  some of them are optional but most of  them are crucial to reach the end and  unless you're some crazy speedrunner  even the optional stuff is almost  required to do  if you want to stand a chance and this  has everything to do with the way the  game scaled its difficulty  nowadays most games give you a chance to  ease into the experience first and then  gradually increase the challenge as you  become better at the game  zelda on the nes didn't do that in fact  it kinda did the opposite  it starts out really hard and then  slowly becomes more doable as you press  on  you get dropped in this huge map for the  time and you're completely left to your  own devices  you have no idea where to go enemies  drain your hp quicker than you can say  and there are no tutorials or practice  stages obviously this kind of game  design wouldn't fly nowadays  since games are now in the mainstream  people like to know what the goal is and  not immediately get their ass handed to  them as soon as they boot up the game  even dark souls which is notorious for  its difficulty in the modern era  still gives you a break in the first  section of the game with enemies that  don't even fight back  giving you a chance to get accustomed to  the controls in the combat it isn't  until about the 20-minute mark that you  encounter your first enemy who doesn't  mess around  the original zelda gives you one screen  one screen of safety not infested by  enemies  from here no matter what direction you  take off in you will immediately run  into your first batch of baddies  and because you only have 3 hearts to  start with 6 hits means  death and even less when being hit by  blue enemies your shield does block  projectiles but not direct hits from the  enemy itself  and some projectiles you can't block  until you buy the magic shield  from the starting point it takes a  minimum of 6 screens to get to the first  level or dungeon and that's if you know  where you're going  6 screens which all include various  types of enemies and like i said most  people on their first playthrough would  have had no idea where they were going  and probably wandered around far  outside those six screens it's no  surprise then that many people died in  just the first few minutes of playing to  make things  a bit more manageable the game's manual  came with a map of the overworld which  was gracious enough to point out the  first four dungeons though it did  obscure some parts of the world to leave  some room for surprises  it also gave tips on how to beat certain  enemies how to find secrets finding  merchants and gathering up your strength  it even spoils all the items you'll be  able to find in the game which  is very unconventional for a zelda game  nowadays can you imagine if twilight  princess straight up told you beforehand  about the spinner or the ball and chain  anyway even with all this information  you are mostly still left in the dark as  secrets aren't exactly straightforward  in this game there is very little that  indicates that a secret is there  no cracks embalmable walls no clear  indication which bush can be burned or  not  not to mention it will take a while  before you even get the tools you need  to find them  once you find your first shop and  realize you can buy say the candle you  first need to roam around and collect  rupees which means dealing with lots of  enemies in your fragile state  the same thing goes for collecting bombs  which you also get from enemies  the overworld starts out insanely hard  and then gets easier  once you start finding and buying more  stuff like the blue ring which decreases  damage taken by half  the magic shield which allows you to  block every type of projectile and more  quality of life improvements like heart  containers and better swords i totally  get why this game scares away players  nowadays  it's such an unconventional approach to  difficulty by modern standards  i myself put this game on the back  burner at least three or four times over  the years  every time i started a new game trying  to get as far as possible and then  getting stumped because i just  couldn't do it once you're outfitted  with better equipment and increase your  hp a couple of times  the overworld becomes manageable for the  most part you get more and more  comfortable just roaming around beating  enemies finding caves and collecting  resources  and the more you do this the easier it  gets to explore and find more so the  difficulty ramp for the overworld itself  looks like this  but if you thought you were in the clear  you are mistaken because the same thing  cannot be said for the game's dungeons  this graph looks more like this the  first 5 dungeons ramp up mildly but then  it hits you with  level 6 which is an absolute nightmare  on your first playthrough and even now  after having beaten the game multiple  times with randomizers included this is  still a part of the game i  always dread and this is mostly due to  these bastards  right here the blue wiz robes if there's  a place in hell for video game enemies  these guys are one of my prime  candidates for banishment to the fiery  pits  these guys are insane they don't take  any back or hits done  are always on the move and they can face  through walls and blocks  and once they have you in your sights  they don't just shoot one projectile  like their orange counterparts  instead they bombard you with no break  in between to get a hit in all the while  they constantly move towards you so even  if you block  every beam you will still get hit by  them if you just stand there  they also have no notable weakness aside  from the sword and can take  quite some abuse and do a lot of damage  themselves what's also funny is that in  this dungeon you find the magic rod  which you would think will make dealing  with them a lot easier  you know like in other zelda games where  the main item is often the key to  beating enemies which were previously a  pain to defeat  but nope the one does absolutely nothing  against them  just great times all around level 7 is  actually a lot easier than 6  and 8 falls slightly below 6 but still  pretty damn hard  but the torture doesn't end there oh no  level 9 takes everything that made 6 and  8 difficult  and puts it on steroids it's the biggest  dungeon of them all  has many and i mean many optional rooms  and secret doorways  making the dungeon one giant maze filled  with monsters  a monster maze if you will to anyone  who's beaten this dungeon without safe  states  i salute you one thing i really  appreciate about this game though is how  finding a dungeon is a really big deal  as mentioned before the locations of the  first four dungeons are hinted at on the  map inside the manual but after that  it's up to you to find the remaining  five and some of these  are hidden very well zelda 2 continued  this trend but starting with a link to  the past  dungeons were no longer a big mystery i  mean they are literally spelled out on  the map and even have a set order  later 3d games while not showing them on  the map do point you in their general  direction  either through your companion or the  story or simply by barring off  everything else in the overworld so by  process of elimination you will get  there no problem which is why for breath  of the wild  i think it's a shame that they didn't go  for more traditional dungeons  if anything this would have been a great  game to hide complex dungeons in the  overworld without anyone telling you  where they are but hey  maybe the sequel will of all classic  zelda games the original two probably  hold up the least  many can still boot up a link to the  past or link's awakening and still enjoy  it a great deal since by that time the  games got more streamlined and easy to  understand  the grandfather of zelda games  definitely isn't the most accessible  game out there  yet the series still owes so much to  this one game it introduced many zelda  staples which we still see today  in some shape or form the tradition of  getting a heart container after a boss  fight the aspect of bombing walls  finding dungeons and getting the items  inside even the classic lore of the hero  princess and ganon all find their origin  here and although  an outdated concept now i think there's  a charm to reading a book that teaches  you things about the world  while you play the game it's almost like  having a survival book with you while  you venture out into the wild  the game was born from miyamoto's  imagination and childhood memories of  exploring a vast world  zelda started out as an open world game  then slowly dipped more and more into  linear territory only to revive the opel  world concept  30 years later in 2017 and look at where  we are now  still looking forward to that new game  in the franchise zelda is one of the  longest running series in gaming history  soon to be 21 games strong not counting  spin-offs and i hope that another 35  years from now  70 year old me will still be celebrating  this franchise and playing or  anticipating the next game  thank you for watching  you

